(window.__CLAIMS_ADD__||function(){})([{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Anthropic, PBC filed a Form S-1 on 2026-10-26 (accession 0001628280-26-067168, accepted 09:00:11).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p1"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The S-1 says it is \"already seeing\" non-technical teams adopt Claude Code and Claude Cowork (U84, F13). It is the company's own claim to investors.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p2"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The sentence before it frames developers as internal champions whose trust leads to wider spread (U84).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p3"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The non-technical claim is unquantified; Cowork adoption is given only relative to Claude Code (U46, F13). Checked: all 4 \"non-technical\" passages and every sentence naming Cowork that contains a figure. None gives a user count for non-technical teams or Cowork.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p4"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The \"non-technical prosumers\" line is in the Prosumers section, about individual Pro and Max subscribers, not employees (U46).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p5"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Risk factor: the law on liability for autonomous AI actions is \"unsettled\" (U85, F13).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p6"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Agency, product-liability and professional-negligence doctrines have not been authoritatively applied to autonomous AI (U85).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p7"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"It is unclear whether an agent's acts bind the user or enterprise, and whether Anthropic's limitation-of-liability and indemnity terms will hold (U85).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p8"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Anthropic may be sued even where the customer set up or supervised the agent (U85; peg hunt row 4).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p9"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Enterprise deployments \"will require broad permissions and deep integrations\", which raises the stakes of any incident (U98).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p10"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Incidents caused by customer misconfiguration or misuse may still bring Anthropic liability exposure (U98).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p11"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The \"AI-generated code\" sentence sits in the OPEN-SOURCE risk factor (copyleft), about Anthropic's OWN use of Claude Code. One follow-on sentence says similar concerns could make enterprises limit use (U86, F13).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p12"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(context, peg hunt) Q3 2026: Enterprise users without Claude Code spent under $75/month; the top 5% using it spent about 20 times that. The full sentence was re-read.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p13"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F1: an Item 1.05 8-K is due four business days after the company DETERMINES the incident is material, not after the breach or its discovery.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a1"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F1: the materiality decision itself must come without unreasonable delay after discovery.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a2"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U47: materiality includes qualitative harm such as reputation, customer relationships and litigation risk (SEC adopting release).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a3"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U47: Director Gerding (21 May 2024) encouraged immaterial or not-yet-assessed incidents to go under another item such as 8.01.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a4"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U87: \"information systems\" means resources \"owned or used by\" the registrant.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a5"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U87: materiality does not depend on where the system sits or who owns it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a6"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F1/U87: \"no exemption for third-party, SaaS, AI or employee-built systems\". The SEC refused a THIRD-PARTY-system exemption. It says nothing about AI tools or employee-built apps. Their coverage follows from \"owned or used by\". It is a sound reading, not an SEC statement.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a7"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F2: Community Bank (subsidiary of CB Financial Services, PA) became aware on 5 May 2026 of an internal incident. Customer data was handled with an unauthorized AI-based application. The filing's exact words are \"artificial intelligence-based software application\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a8"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F2: it was judged material on 7 May because of the data's volume and sensitivity.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a9"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F2: names, SSNs and dates of birth were disclosed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a10"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F2: there was no operational disruption and no material financial impact expected.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a11"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F2: signed and filed 11 May 2026 (EDGAR records the filing date as 2026-05-11). That is two business days after the 7 May determination, inside the four-day limit.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a12"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F2 trap: the filing does not name the tool, give a count or settle the root cause. It does say state and federal notices are under way.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a13"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F2/U71: is CB Financial the ONLY incident 8-K naming an unauthorized AI tool? EDGAR full-text search over 8-Ks filed 2025-01-01 to 2026-10-27. \"Item 1.05\" + \"artificial intelligence\" gave 3 hits, and only CB Financial is an Item 1.05 filing. \"Material Cybersecurity Incidents\" + \"artificial intelligence\" gave 9 hits, again only CB Financial under 1.05. \"unauthorized\" + \"artificial intelligence\" + \"Item 1.05\" gave 2 hits, one of them CB. \"AI-generated code\" or \"shadow IT\" with \"cybersecurity incident\" gave 0 hits. \"AI tool\", \"generative AI\" and \"AI agent\" with \"cybersecurity incident\" gave only earnings releases, merger exhibits and risk-factor boilerplate; no Item 8.01 incident report names an AI tool. Limit: full-text search sees only literal wording, so an incident described without these words would be missed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a14"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U71: Debevoise tracker (law firm, secondary): from 21 May 2024 to 21 May 2026, 29 issuers filed Item 1.05 and 50 filed Item 8.01; 5 filed both.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a15"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U71: \"Item 1.05 filings rarely say how the attacker got in\". The saved tracker text has no passage on attack vectors or root cause.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-a16"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F3: NY SHIELD puts the security duty on whoever owns or licenses New Yorkers' private data. The text has no approval or who-built-it qualifier. Statute text is from FindLaw because nysenate.gov returned 403.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a17"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F3: SHIELD's example safeguards include software-design risk and employee training. They are examples of compliance (\"such as\"), not mandates.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a18"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F3: only the AG enforces SHIELD; there is no private right of action.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a19"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F3: small businesses get a scaled standard.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a20"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F3 trap: the $5,000 SHIELD penalty figure. The text routes penalties through GBL 350-d (quoted in A19), but 350-d was not fetched, so the dollar amount is unchecked.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-a21"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U48: NY 899-aa: a data owner must notify affected residents within 30 days of discovering the breach.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a22"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U48: \"notify the AG within 30 days\". The AG notice (with the Dept. of State and State Police, plus DFS for covered entities) is triggered whenever residents are notified and covers timing, content and the number affected. The clause read sets no separate 30-day clock for the AG.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a23"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U48: a business that only maintains someone else's data must tell the owner immediately (and within 30 days).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a24"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U48: for knowing or reckless failure to notify, the penalty is the greater of $5,000 or up to $20 per failed notice, with the per-notice amount capped at $250,000.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a25"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"NEW (not in the dossier, bears on the CB-type case): NY waives individual notice for an INADVERTENT disclosure by staff authorized to see the data, if the business reasonably finds misuse unlikely. It must document that in writing, and if over 500 New Yorkers are affected, give it to the AG within 10 days.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a26"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U27/F3: California 1798.81.5(b): any business that owns, licenses or maintains Californians' personal information must keep reasonable security. From FindLaw because leginfo returned 403.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a27"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U27/F3: CCPA 1798.150 gives \"$100 to $750 per consumer per incident\". The statute's printed figures are inflation-adjusted, and the CPPA set them at $107 to $799 from 1 Jan 2025. The suit also requires that unencrypted data was taken or disclosed BECAUSE of the security failure.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a28"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U27: the private suit turns on the business's breach of its OWN security duty.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a29"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F4: CPPA first audit reports are due 1 Apr 2028 (2026 revenue over $100M), 1 Apr 2029 ($50M–$100M) and 1 Apr 2030 (under $50M).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a30"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F4: the audit components include software inventories, allowlisting and approval processes that block unauthorized devices.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a31"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F4: secure coding with code review and testing is an audit component.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a32"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F4: \"large data processors\". The rule applies only where processing \"presents significant risk\": a business earning most of its revenue from selling or sharing data, OR a CCPA business that processed 250,000+ consumers' data or 50,000+ consumers' sensitive data. Components are assessed \"if applicable\", and the text never names AI-built tools or shadow IT. Read from the CPPA's approved-text PDF; that it matches the OAL-filed final was not checked.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a33"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F5: Van Buren (decided 3 June 2021): \"exceeds authorized access\" is about areas you cannot reach, not about improper purpose.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a34"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U88: footnote 8 left open whether contracts or policies can set the access limits.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a35"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F5: DOJ's policy (Justice Manual 9-48.000, updated May 2022) requires the access line to be drawn in code or configuration, not policy, before charging \"exceeds authorized access\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a36"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F5: \"DOJ will not prosecute employees for breaching a workplace use policy\". The policy is narrower: its example is an employee misusing a computer assigned for their exclusive use, and the bar covers only the \"exceeds authorized access\" theory. It is internal guidance that creates no enforceable rights.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a37"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U28/F6: West Technology Group and CX360's suit against a former salesman (D. Conn., No. 3:24-cv-00178, filed 8 Feb 2024): Otter recordings, DTSA claims. The source is a law-firm article (secondary). The plaintiffs called Otter \"unauthorized\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a38"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U28/F6: the case settled by November 2024. Source: Law360 case page, secondary; coverage dated 15 Nov 2024.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a39"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U28/F6: the order to return documents and swear to it, and the 200+ forwarded emails. Seen only in a search-engine summary of Law360 headlines, not in any saved text.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-a40"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U72/F6: Samsung (reported 2 May 2023) banned generative AI on company devices and networks and warned of discipline up to firing. No named firing was reported. Source: news site, secondary.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a41"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U49/F3: NY AG v. Wegmans (30 June 2022): $400,000; more than 3 million consumers and more than 830,000 New Yorkers; containers open from January 2018 (and a second from November 2018) to 2021; failure to inventory and test cloud assets.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a42"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U38/F21: Reuters (27 Aug 2026, via KFGO): MSIG, QBE and Beazley are reviewing cyber wording, mostly clarifying rather than excluding; targeted exclusions are discussed in \"some pockets\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a43"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U38: QBE says an AI-triggered conventional incident stays inside cyber cover.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a44"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U97: Verisk names two exclusion candidates: systemic events, and an agent \"acting as designed\" that some insurers may class as non-cyber. The \"little claims data\" part was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a45"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U39/F21: ISO's generative-AI exclusions (CG 40 47 and two narrower forms) are for GENERAL LIABILITY, available from January 2026. A review counted 2,369 records in 49 states, and no admitted standalone replacement was found. Source: trade press, secondary.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a46"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U39: \"Berkley, Chubb, Travelers, Berkshire Hathaway and AIG reportedly filed to adopt ISO forms or equivalents by April\". Confirmed as the article's statement only, with no filings seen.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a47"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U105/F21: W. R. Berkley's \"absolute\" AI exclusion for D&O, E&O and fiduciary products. Source: Hunton blog, secondary. Note the post is dated 28 May 2025, not 2026.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a48"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U66: Coalition's affirmative AI endorsement (26 Mar 2024) for U.S. surplus and Canada cyber policies, covering an \"AI security event\" and deepfake funds-transfer fraud.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a49"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U66: Beazley confirmed affirmative AI cover in cyber and tech E&O on 17 Sept 2026 (trade press).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a50"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U66/F21 scope trap: Beazley's \"affirmative AI\" wording covers ATTACKER-side AI. It is silent on a client's own AI use. \"Affirmative AI cover\" therefore does not mean a staff-built tool's failure is covered. Source: trade-press analysis, secondary.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a51"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F21 synthesis: \"shifting but not uniformly excluding\". The cyber market is mostly clarifying; ISO's general-liability forms and Berkley's management-liability form exclude broadly; Coalition and Beazley add cover.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a52"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F6: no case was found of an employee sued or fired over an app they BUILT. This was not re-searched; it remains the dossier's search absence.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a53"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F13: the S-1 was \"keyword-searched, not read in full\". This pass full-text-searched the whole saved filing for every term above. It has still not been read cover to cover.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a54"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F7/U16: IBM 2025, one in five organisations reported a breach due to shadow AI. Denominator is the 600 breached organisations, not all companies.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b1"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F7/U16: organisations with HIGH shadow-AI use averaged about $670,000 more in breach costs than those with low or none. A comparison among already-breached firms; correlational.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b2"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F7: 63% of breached organisations had no AI governance policy or were still developing one; of those with policies, 34% audited regularly for unsanctioned AI.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b3"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U16: shadow-AI incidents in 2025 compromised more PII (65%) and IP (40%) than the global average (53%, 33%).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b4"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U50: IBM 2025 method (600 breached orgs, March 2024 to February 2025; Ponemon ran it, IBM sponsored and analysed) and the shadow-AI definition. The method is right. The dossier's wording of the definition (\"workers downloading or using unapproved internet-based AI tools\") is NOT in the saved IBM release, which says only \"unregulated, unauthorized use of AI\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b5"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F8/U29: IBM 2026, security incidents involving shadow AI more than doubled to 43% from 20%. ★ Now confirmed in IBM's own report, not only in Baker Donelson / Kiteworks / Varonis. IBM's 2026 definition: workers using unapproved AI. Note the wording shift: 2025 said \"a breach due to shadow AI\", 2026 says \"security incidents involving\" shadow AI; IBM itself presents them as one series.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b6"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U29: shadow-AI incidents cost an average of $5.39M in 2026 vs $4.63M the prior year. This is the average cost of breaches involving shadow AI, not a high-vs-low gap like 2025's $670K; the two cost figures are not the same measure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b7"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F8: the 2026 global average breach cost is a record $4.99M, up 12%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b8"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F8 said \"68% of AI-incident organizations lacked AI governance policies\". → IBM says 68% of breached organisations (all of them, not only those with an AI incident) lacked AI governance, up from 63%; IBM's own chart splits it into 35% no policy and 33% still developing. Say \"no policy or still writing one\", never \"68% have no policy\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b9"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U29: only 19% reported governance and security teams coordinating.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b10"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U29: 49% of shadow-AI incidents led to data loss or compromise and about 21% to a regulatory fine. The dossier had these from Varonis alone; IBM's report carries both (fines: \"about one in five incidents\"; 21% in Figure 30).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b11"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U51: IBM 2026 method. 602 breached organisations, March 2025 to February 2026, 17 industries, 16 countries and regions, 3,558 interviews; May 2026 follow-up with 456 of the 602. The unit of analysis is the organisation, and every one of them had a breach.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b12"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U63/F8: IBM's separate \"AI-related breach\" category (attacks ON AI models or apps) rose to 21% from 13%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b13"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U63: of organisations with an AI-related breach, 92% lacked proper AI access controls (2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b14"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U63: the 2025 equivalents were 13% and 97%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b15"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U63/F8: IBM attributes AI-related breaches mostly to weaknesses around the model (APIs, apps, plug-ins, cloud misconfiguration), not to AI-written code.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b16"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"NEW, not in the dossier: IBM 2026 also prices \"shadow IT\" (no visibility into how many apps exist and where) as a factor that adds about $201,165 to the $4.99M average, measured in isolation. The closest IBM figure to the business-built-app problem.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b17"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F9: Veracode 2025 (vendor that sells code scanners) tested 100+ models on 80 curated tasks; vulnerabilities in 45% of cases. Raw-model code completion, no agent, no review.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b18"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U52: 2025 by language and flaw: Java over 70% failure, others 38-45%; XSS failed 86%, log injection 88%; larger models no better.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b19"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F9: Spring 2026 update, about 55% secure while syntax exceeds 95%; flat since 2023.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b20"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F9: Spring 2026 by language: Python 62, C# 58, JavaScript 57, Java 29. (The July release gives Python 63 and Java 30: quote the edition you cite.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b21"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F9: Spring 2026 by flaw type: SQL injection 82, crypto 86, XSS 15, log injection 13. Only four flaw types are tested.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b22"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F9: the reasoning-model exception, 70-72% for GPT-5 with extended reasoning.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b23"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F9/U35: July 2026 report, 56% average across four snapshots, 11 leaderboard models, GPT-5.5 top at 68%, reasoning 56% vs non-reasoning 51%, tested with no security prompting. Model counts differ by page (100+ here, \"over 150\" in spring).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b24"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U0/F10: Pearce et al., 89 Copilot scenarios, 1,689 programs, about 40% vulnerable (2021 Copilot, prepared scenarios).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b25"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U0: Pearce venue is IEEE S&P 2022.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b26"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U1/F10: Perry et al. (ACM CCS 2023): 47 analysed participants, 33 with AI and 14 control; AI group less secure on four of five tasks.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b27"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U1/F10: the AI group was more likely to believe its code was secure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b28"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U1/F10: SQL injection, 36% of the AI group vs 7% of controls (p=0.041).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b29"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U15/U53/F10: Sandoval et al., N=58 student programmers, a C linked list, code-cushman-001; AI users' critical bugs no more than 10% above control. (USENIX Security 2023 venue per the dossier; not in the saved arXiv text.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b30"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U2/F10: SusVibes (ICML 2026, PMLR 306), 186 real-repository tasks, 12 agent settings; SWE-Agent + Claude 4 Sonnet 57% correct, 11.8% secure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b31"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U73/F10: the 2026 arXiv audit, 9,041 apps collected, 200 deployed apps audited, 1,186 vulnerabilities, 91.0% with at least one, 65.77% Critical or High. Preprint (v4, revised 14 Sep 2026); the abstract page shows no journal or venue.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b32"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U77: Georgia Tech's radar, 74 CVEs attributed to AI-authored code out of 43,849 advisories (to 20 March 2026); the researcher calls it a lower bound, likely 5 to 10 times higher.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b33"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U77: \"a December 2025 preprint finds AI code clusters in boilerplate … defects persist longer\". Not in the saved Register article; no preprint identified or opened.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b34"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U41/F11: RedAccess's own summary (vendor selling shadow-AI tools; report gated, only the landing page read): 380K assets, 5K corporate, 40% of those exposing sensitive data (about 2,000).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b35"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F11: the funnel is described differently in each retelling. WIRED via PPC Land says 5,000+ had essentially no authentication; Security Boulevard says about 5,000 contained sensitive corporate information; RedAccess says 5K were \"built for corporate purposes\". Only the ~2,000 is stable across accounts.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b36"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U95: Wix (Base44) disputed the examples; WIRED could not confirm the data was real; RedAccess says owners confirmed exposure for a few dozen apps. (Secondary: PPC Land relaying WIRED; WIRED not fetched.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b37"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U104/F11: Replit's CEO says public apps being reachable is expected behaviour and privacy is one click.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b38"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U104/U95: RedAccess contacted Replit less than 24 hours before the press; Base44 and Lovable say no URLs were supplied.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b39"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U106: platforms named are Lovable, Replit, Base44 and Netlify; found by Google and Bing searches on the platforms' default domains; no exploitation. The Hacker News piece is a RedAccess partner contribution, not reporting.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b40"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U106: \"RedAccess says the work grew out of shadow-AI research for its customers\". Not found in the saved Hacker News, PPC Land or Security Boulevard text.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b41"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U78/F11: Escape.tech (29 Oct 2025): 5,600+ public apps (about 4,000 from Lovable), 2,000+ vulnerabilities, 400+ exposed secrets, 175 PII instances; passive scan, a lower bound.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b42"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U78 called Escape's scan \"the only independent\" one. → It is independent of RedAccess, but Escape is itself a security-scanning vendor publishing on its own blog, with a Lovable-heavy sample. Say \"a second vendor's scan\", not \"independent\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b43"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F12: July 2025, Replit's agent deleted the production database of SaaStr's founder.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b44"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U40: Replit's CEO called it unacceptable, offered a refund and a postmortem.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b45"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U40: the agent lacked access to the proper internal docs; fixes were a forced docs search, dev/prod separation, and staging \"in the works\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b46"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U40: \"planning-only/chat mode was announced as in progress\". Not in the saved Register text.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b47"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U24/F12: Replit's 21 July 2025 post launched dev/prod database separation in beta for new apps, with existing apps migrated later. The post does not mention the SaaStr incident (neither the founder's name nor \"SaaStr\" appears in the saved text).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b48"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U25/F12: Lovable, 3 Feb to 20 Apr 2026, public projects' chat history and source code reachable by any Lovable user with a project link; private projects and Lovable Cloud not affected.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b49"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U25/F12: researcher reports through HackerOne (the first on 22 Feb 2026) were closed without escalation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b50"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U25: a fix within two hours; all current public projects being made private.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b51"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U25/F12: \"Lovable first denied a breach and called the visibility intentional\". Lovable's own post admits only that its initial response \"missed the mark\" and that its triage documents described the behaviour as intended; the denial itself is not in the saved primary (it rests on press accounts not opened here). Say \"Lovable's first public response was wrong, by its own account\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b52"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U26/F12: Replit's terms (last updated 3 Aug 2026): the user is liable for their apps and access permissions.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b53"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U26/F12: Replit's terms say AI code may be erroneous or incomplete.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b54"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U26: Replit disclaims direct and indirect damages, service as is; no dollar cap appears in the saved terms.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b55"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U26: Replit users indemnify Replit.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b56"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U26: Lovable's terms (last updated 28 Aug 2026) make the user responsible for Customer Data and the apps they build.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b57"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U26/F12 said Lovable caps liability at 12 months' fees \"except for gross negligence or willful misconduct\". → The 12-month cap's only exception is liability that law forbids limiting. The gross-negligence carve-out attaches to a different sentence: Lovable's exclusion for LOSS OF CUSTOMER DATA.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b58"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U26: Lovable disclaims AI-output errors and requires user indemnification.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b59"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U44: the researcher's CVE-2025-48757 timeline. Lovable confirmed receipt 24 Mar 2025; a \"security scan\" shipped 24 Apr 2025 checks only that an RLS policy exists; CVE published 29 May 2025 with no remediation or user notification.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b60"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U44: the researcher works at Replit, a competitor, so the account is not neutral.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b61"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U44: the NVD record carries Lovable's \"disputed\" note, placing responsibility on customers. The dossier could not confirm it; the NVD API record confirms it (tags: disputed, exclusively-hosted-service).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b62"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"NEW, not in the dossier: the researcher's March 2025 scan found inadequate RLS on 170 of 1,645 Lovable showcase projects (about 10.3%). Deployed apps, one platform, one flaw type, a competitor's scan.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b63"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U9: JPMorgan's VaR spreadsheet divided by the sum of the old and new rates instead of their average; the task force says this likely muted volatility by a factor of two and lowered the VaR, but the amount, reach and start date are unclear. Found after the May 2012 re-review.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c1"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U10: the Model Review Group called the manual spreadsheet process error prone, required automation by 31 Jan 2012, and nobody checked; it never happened. Checked version: the model's builder was relied on to OPERATE it, but daily data entry was done by an employee who reported to him (the dossier's \"builder ran it day to day\" is slightly off).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c2"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U90: the Basel II.5 VaR model ran on hand-filled Excel sheets fed by copy and paste. Checked version: this became apparent during the Model Review Group's 2011 approval review (the report's account), not something the 2012-13 task force itself spotted at the time.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c3"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U60: a spreadsheet error made the 10 April 2012 VaR miss that day's ~$400m loss; fixed, treated as a one-off, no wider inquiry; Investment Bank staff noticed first. Checked timing: this was AFTER the 'London Whale' press articles but before the firm's 10 May disclosure, so 'before the losses went public' needs that qualifier.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c4"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F16 trap: the task force put principal blame for the London Whale on the trading strategy, not the spreadsheet; losses reached about $5.8bn by 30 June 2012.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c5"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U91: remedial measures name spreadsheet controls only within CIO's valuation group; the firm-wide fix was a risk self-assessment (VaR-driver spot check, model approval extended to implementation and monitoring). No firm-wide end-user-computing policy: the saved report has no hit for 'end-user' or 'end user'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c6"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U11: Herndon, Ash and Pollin (UMass PERI WP322, April 2013) find over-90%-debt growth of 2.2%, not RR's -0.1%, and 'not dramatically different' from lower-debt growth. Their calculation, their preferred weighting.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c7"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U12: the Excel error alone (dropping Australia, Austria, Belgium, Canada, Denmark) moves the over-90% figure from 2.2 to 1.9; year exclusions plus equal country weighting account for almost 2 points; spreadsheet plus transcription errors add about 0.4.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c8"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U99: Reinhart and Rogoff conceded the coding error ('full stop') and a notable change in the over-90% average, defended their median, and rejected the 'selective exclusion' charge and the weighting critique.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c9"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U13 / U32: Panko (EuSpRIG 2000) compiles seven field audits: errors in 24% of 367 spreadsheets overall (older audits weak), at least 86% in the recent better audits; the three most thorough audits found cell error rates of 1.1%, 2.2%, 2.5%. Note the 367 is dominated by a 1992 tax audit of 273 sheets that only counted errors big enough to change tax due.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c10"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U61: Panko (EuSpRIG 2015) tables 85 operational spreadsheets from six inspection studies: errors in 94% (weighted). Checked version: the '1% to 5%' is the base error rate from human-error research, not a cell error rate measured in those audits; only two of the six studies reported cell error rates (1.2%; 2.2% and 2.5%). He also says developers and firms are overconfident.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c11"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U20: PCAOB staff guidance (23 Jan 2009) defines end-user computing controls over spreadsheets and other user-developed applications and lists auditor tests (access, formula / query logic, completeness and accuracy, backups). It is staff views, not Board rules.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c12"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U21: PCAOB Staff Audit Practice Alert 11 (24 Oct 2013): inspectors saw firms that knew some IT general controls were ineffective yet did not test the report writers and systems producing spreadsheets, queries or reports.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c13"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U22: TransAct Technologies' FY2019 10-K: material weakness over key spreadsheets (plus one over Oracle user access); no material misstatement; remediation = standard Oracle reports or a validation control; ICFR audited by PwC.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c14"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U67: Comverse's 10-K for FY ended 31 Jan 2011 lists end-user computing controls as a material weakness (access, change management, validation of spreadsheets). Context the dossier leaves out: it was one of a long list of weaknesses in a filing made late because of them, not a stand-alone spreadsheet story.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c15"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U103: PCAOB staff's July 2024 GenAI spotlight. Checked version: only SOME preparers said GenAI governance resembled that of other technology; OTHERS said they were writing new policies. Accounting use was secondary to operational and customer-facing use. No control failure reported.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c16"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U43: Microsoft's Deutsche Bahn case study (VENDOR CASE STUDY, Microsoft Learn, dated 2025-04-10): 4,000 citizen developers, 500+ apps in production, handwriting errors 20% to 2%. The page has no hit for retired, orphaned, failed or incident.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c17"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U33: Forrester TEI of Power Platform (VENDOR-COMMISSIONED, Microsoft, July 2024): 8 interviewees, 7 organisations, one composite; 'reduced shadow IT' is listed only as unquantified; one energy company ran 4 people on strategy plus 8 on governance. The headline 224% ROI is a modelled composite, not a measured programme.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c18"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U74: Gartner (ANALYST FORECAST, Oct 2009) said at least a quarter of new business apps would be built by citizen developers by 2014, defined as building outside IT and its governance. Read only through dated trade-press relay (ITPro, 23 Oct 2009), not at Gartner.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c19"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U30: Gartner (ANALYST FORECAST) that citizen developers would be at least 80% of low-code platform users by 2026, up from 60% in 2021. Read only via SD Times (9 March 2023, updated 14 July 2023); the dossier's 'Dec 2022 Gartner release' was not read. Low-code users, not vibe coders.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c20"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U42 / U80: Gartner, 'Govern Vibe Coding for Citizen Developers With Self-Service Platforms' (29 June 2026), Strategic Planning Assumptions (ANALYST FORECASTS): by 2028, 60% of vibe-coded apps retired for failing to deliver predictable business value; 30% of software engineering teams under material capacity strain from maintaining citizen-built vibe-coded apps. Read at Gartner's licensed reprint.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c21"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U80: the same Gartner note says business users are demanding Lovable, Replit, Claude Code and Codex. Analyst assertion; no figure attached.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c22"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U42: the note is distributed by Tray.ai, a vendor of integration and governance platforms (page published 2026-09-02).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c23"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U82: the '89% drop in unauthorized use when sanctioned tools are provided' comes from one vendor report (Unseen Security) cited in a CSA research note (30 May 2026); the note states no sample or method for it. Trap: the same note carries a DIFFERENT 89% (AI usage invisible to security teams), from Help Net Security.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c24"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U96: Binzer and Winkler's multivocal review (ICSOB 2022) says citizen-development research is still scarce and lists governance as one of six themes. The second review (Subotica) was not opened, and 'no independent study measuring outcomes exists' is an absence claim from the finder's search, not a checked fact.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c25"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U92: Power Platform data policies (DLP) are off until an admin creates them and cannot tell dev from prod connections. Checked version: they are not purely connector-level any more: 'connector action control' can allow or block individual actions; but Microsoft-owned standard connectors and Dataverse (CDS) connectors cannot be blocked at all. Page dated 2025-08-29.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c26"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U92: every employee can reach the default environment, and the CoE sharing-audit process runs only after sharing.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c27"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U100: orphaned apps and flows. Checked version: Microsoft says orphaned resources CAN pose a security risk; it does not call them 'recurring', and gives no counts. The daily clean-up check is a CoE Starter Kit component (the kit now unmaintained, C29).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c28"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U101: the CoE Starter Kit is no longer actively maintained; core capabilities moved into the Power Platform admin center; issues no longer reviewed. Page metadata: ms.date 2026-10-17, updatedat 2026-10-19. ('Most often cited' is the finder's characterisation, unchecked.)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c29"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U102: Agent 365 is Microsoft's agent registry and control plane (Microsoft and non-Microsoft agents); viewing the inventory needs a role (AI Reader or AI Administrator) but no specific licence; applying Conditional Access or identity governance to agents needs Entra Agent ID licensing.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c30"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U23: UK NCSC blog (by an NCSC Principal Security Architect, 18 June 2026): full vibe coding can be fine for prototypes, demos and limited-exposure internal tools; slide toward manual for authentication, sensitive personal data, secrets. Written for software builders generally, not specifically business teams.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c31"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U94: NCSC's CTO for Architecture (24 March 2026; the dossier's 'modified 30 April' not seen on the saved page): non-technical vibe coders get output that is often unreliable, hard to maintain or critically flawed; current risks likely far outside most organisations' tolerance; unreviewed AI code in production will grow over five years.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c32"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U68: 'Careful adoption of agentic AI services' (ASD's ACSC, CISA, NSA, Canada, NZ, UK NCSC). Checked version: it goes further than 'start with low-risk tasks': its opening says organisations should ONLY use agentic AI for low-risk, non-sensitive tasks; never broad access; no high-impact actions without prior human approval; governance and oversight are prerequisites.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c33"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U68: CISA's own page lists the guidance with publish date May 01, 2026 (co-authored with Australia's ACSC). Oddity: as fetched today the CISA page's title and blurb say 'Agentic SI' / 'super intelligence' while the document says agentic AI; cite the document's title. Advice, not regulation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c34"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U69: UK ICO Tech Futures (agentic AI): DPO oversight gets harder when staff experiment with agents; shadow or short-lived agents compound it; controller-processor obligations still apply.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c35"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U69: on 8 Oct 2026 the ICO launched an agentic-AI call for evidence (closes 20 Nov 2026) and confirmed enquiries with OpenAI, Anthropic, Meta and the UK AI Security Institute.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c36"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U70: EU Digital Omnibus on AI = Regulation (EU) 2026/1744; EP plenary 16 June, Council 29 June, signed 8 July, OJ 24 July 2026; high-risk duties delayed to 2 Dec 2027 (stand-alone) and 2 Aug 2028 (embedded).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c37"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U74 / F18: did Gartner's 2009 '25% of new business apps by 2014' come true? No measurement of the outcome found in the saved relays or one targeted search (which turned up only the 2009 and 2011 repetitions of the forecast).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-c38"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U54/F14: Census BTOS, share of US businesses using AI in the past two weeks hovered at 17-20%, Dec 2025 to May 2026. Firm-level, not worker-level.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U54: 37% of firms with 250+ employees.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U54: Information sector 39.7% vs national 19.8% (as of May 3, 2026).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d3"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U54/F14 trap: Census changed the question in November 2025, so pre-November comparisons break.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d4"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U55: Census working paper CES 26-25 (= NBER w35141), AI supplement Nov 2025-Jan 2026: 18% of firms, 32% employment-weighted.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d5"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U55: among adopters, Sales and Marketing 52%, Strategy 45%, IT 41%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d6"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U55: 66% only augment; AI-linked employment decreases in only 2% of firms.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d7"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U55/F14: \"worker use happening 'without formal adoption'\". The idea is right but the dossier's quote is not verbatim; Census's words are \"without formal firmlevel adoption\". The paper also quantifies it, which the dossier missed: in 36% of firms where workers use AI there is no formal firm-level adoption. This is the closest official number to \"shadow AI\", but it covers any AI use, not building software.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d8"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(added) The 36% shadow-use figure, from the full paper.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d9"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U3/F14: \"software coding or debugging\" is one of the listed worker tasks, and specialised tasks incl. software debugging show lower incidence than writing (85% of firms with worker use) and information search (50%). The coding share itself sits only in Figure 9 (a chart, no number in the text), and BTOS is answered by the firm, so it cannot separate engineers from non-engineers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d10"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U4/F14: Anthropic's own Claude Code study: ~400,000 sessions from ~235,000 people, Oct 2025-Apr 2026. Anthropic's data on its own product's users; airs as plain fact, labelled as Anthropic's.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d11"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U4/F14: after Computer and Mathematical, the next largest group is Business and Financial Operations; fastest-growing non-software groups are management, sales, legal. Occupation inferred for only ~70% of sessions.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d12"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U4/F14: verified success 29% (non-software) vs 34% (software). True only for code-producing sessions; across all sessions it is 26% vs 30% (ep46 T3-5 used the all-sessions pair; both are in the source, no contradiction). F14 drops the \"code-producing\" qualifier; the script must keep it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d13"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U4/F14: the study gives no share of non-engineer users or sessions (it ranks groups only). Nothing found gives that share.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-d14"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(added) The study's own limit: it cannot tell whether what was built was kept or used.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d15"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U56: Anthropic Economic Index (June 2026, \"Cadences\"): code and technical work like apps or scripts is about a sixth of chat and Cowork conversations. A share of conversations, not of people.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d16"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U56/F14: the companion survey (~9,700) is tech-heavy: Computer and Math ~30% of respondents vs 4% of US employment.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d17"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U62/F14: Stack Overflow 2025, \"72% of professional developers say they are not vibe coding\". Corrected on two points: the 72% is of all respondents to the question (26,564), not professional developers only; and Stack Overflow adds a further 5% \"emphatic\" no, so about 77% say no. Some form of yes (\"Yes, emphatically\" 0.4 + \"Yes\" 11.9 + \"Yes, somewhat\" 2.8) is about 15% (our sum).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d18"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U62: 52% don't use agents or stick to simpler AI tools.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d19"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U75/F14: Microsoft (company claim), Nadella on the FY2024 Q4 call, Tuesday July 30, 2024: 48M monthly active Power Platform users, +40% y/y. Now read from Microsoft's own transcript (the dossier had a Siasat relay). Counts users of the product family, not builders, and is over two years old.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d20"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(added, company claim) Same call: 480,000 organisations had used Power Platform's AI features. Organisations, not people.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d21"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U14: MIT NANDA's 95% \"zero return\" is a preliminary report built on 300+ public initiatives, 52 interviews and 153 conference surveys, about enterprise GenAI pilots in general, not business-team building. Same rating as ep46 T3-32/33.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d22"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U14: its shadow-AI line: personal AI use at 90%+ of companies vs 40% with an official subscription. Weak, non-random sample.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d23"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U36: the one non-programmer study found is a small arXiv case study: non-programmers built a basic web app with AI help, mostly finished, and liked it. No follow-up on use, maintenance or security.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d24"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U36: \"a vendor survey of 50 executives: 50% of enterprises have non-engineering teams building internal apps with AI\". Not opened; no source named in the dossier. Do not air.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-d25"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U34/F15: YipitData (a paid data vendor; its own published insight) e-receipt panel: 1,720 US panelists with a Lovable receipt Sept 2025-Jan 2026; 168 had one again in May-Aug 2026, 1,552 did not.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d26"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U34: about two-thirds of lapsers had no other paid-AI receipt in the tracked set.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d27"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(added, F15 trap) YipitData's own caveat: annual or credit billing can make a paying customer look lapsed. So \"only 168 of 1,720 still pay\" is a receipt count, not a churn rate. Consumer card data, not enterprise use.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d28"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U76/F15: Barclays traffic data (via Business Insider, Sept 2025): Lovable about -40%, v0 -64% since May, Bolt.new -27%. Read only in a dev.ua rewrite; the Business Insider original could not be fetched (blocked), and Barclays' note was not seen. Search snippets also mention Vercel attributing part of v0's drop to bot blocking and Lovable's CEO disputing the figures; neither was saved.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d29"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U76: Bolt.new's CEO said churn is very high across the sector (quote via the same rewrite, translated).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d30"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U81/F15: Lovable (company statement): a co-founder, on stage at HumanX, Amsterdam, Sept 24, 2026: annualised revenue past $600M, up from about $500M in June. Run-rate, not audited revenue. Two secondary reports agree (KuCoin reproducing CoinDesk; Siècle Digital); no Lovable primary saved.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d31"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U81: same figure from an independent outlet (French).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d32"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U17/F23: METR RCT (early 2025): 16 experienced open-source developers, 246 real issues; AI-allowed tasks took 19% longer. Speed only, early-2025 tools.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d33"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U17/F23: they forecast a 24% speedup and afterwards believed 20%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d34"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U18/F23: METR follow-up (Feb 24, 2026): returning developers -18% time (CI -38% to +9%), new developers -4% (CI -15% to +9%). Both intervals include zero.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d35"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U18: why METR calls it unreliable: developers declined to work without AI, pay cut from $150 to $50/hr, timing breaks with concurrent agents.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d36"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U18/F23: METR thinks developers are probably faster now, but its data is very weak evidence of how much.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d37"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U64/F23: Anthropic's own RCT: 52 mostly junior engineers learning the Trio library; AI group 50% vs 67% on the quiz (p = 0.01). Anthropic's study; airs as plain fact, labelled.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d38"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U64: the AI group was about two minutes faster, not statistically significant.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d39"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U83/F23: \"DORA-type data shows individual output up while organizational delivery stays flat\". Wrong attribution. That line is Faros AI's own telemetry (a vendor, July 2025, 10,000+ developers), not DORA.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d40"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U83: what DORA 2025 (Google, survey of ~5,000) actually says: AI adoption goes with higher throughput and product performance, and lower stability. Matches ep46 T4-38.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d41"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U83: even Faros now says the \"flat\" finding is out of date: its 2026 report finds organisational throughput gains (vendor claim).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d42"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U5/U8/U19/F22: Stanford \"Canaries\" (revised Aug 12, 2026; ADP through June 2026): 22-25s in AI-exposed jobs 19% below the kept-pace level; no comparable gap for experienced workers. Same as ep46 C1.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d43"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U5: the same measure was 15% at the July 2025 vintage.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d44"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U8: \"the earlier 2025 version reported a 13% relative decline\". True, but that was a regression estimate, not the same measure as the 19%; don't say \"13% → 19%\" (ep46 C3).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d45"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U5: in levels, top-two exposure quintiles -11% for 22-25s (Nov 2022-June 2026), bottom three +10%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d46"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U5: it works through hiring, not more separations.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d47"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U19/F22: it persists without tech firms and computer jobs and with rate and remote-work controls; it weakens with education, shows pre-trends, and the authors call it descriptive, not causal.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d48"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U6/F22: ACS 2024 shows a much smaller gap than ADP (-0.022, CI spans zero, vs -0.132).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d49"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U6: the CPS has only 23-48 young software developers a month, so its estimates swing 20%+ month to month.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d50"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U6/F22: the ADP sample over-represents AI-exposed occupations.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d51"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U89: software developers appear only in appendix case studies (Fig. B.3), with no number in the text; pay for 22-25 developers grew somewhat faster after ChatGPT, possibly a composition effect.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d52"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U57/F22: Dallas Fed (Atkinson and Yamco, Jan 6, 2026; CPS): share of 20-24s in the most-exposed occupations 16.4% (Nov 2022) to 15.5% (Sept 2025).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d53"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U57: driven by fewer people entering work, not layoffs; at most 0.1 pt on aggregate unemployment; may not be causal (education).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d54"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(added, script trap) The Dallas Fed's \"most AI-exposed\" group is not coders: its top examples are retail supervisors, secretaries and customer-service reps. Don't use it as a tech-jobs number.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d55"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U65/F22: NY Fed (Emanuel, Harrington, Pallais, June 2026): remote work explains 64% of the rise in young-graduate unemployment, a back-of-envelope figure for 2017-19 to 2022-24 (ep46 §0 #10); software engineering is their \"remotable\" example.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d56"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U65: under-29 graduates 3.1% (2017-19) to 3.7% (2022-25).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d57"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U93/F22: Fed Board FEDS 2026-018 (Crane and Soto, March 23, 2026): coder employment decelerated sharply after ChatGPT, not explained by slowing industries; an occupation-specific shock.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d58"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(added, script trap) Same abstract: coder employment is still growing, just more slowly. \"Coders are losing jobs\" is not this paper's finding.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d59"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U37/F22: Indeed Hiring Lab (July 30, 2025): no smoking gun; nearly half the net fall in tech postings from the peak came before ChatGPT; AI may be holding back a rebound.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d60"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U37: tech postings -36% vs Feb 2020 on July 11, 2025; software engineers -49% (early 2025). Dated: Indeed's software-development index on FRED was 78.9 on 2026-10-16 (about 21% below Feb 2020; the same series ep46 uses; the FRED value 78.86 was read by hand).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d61"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U45/F22: Pragmatic Engineer (a newsletter, opinion; Feb 20, 2025): interest rates explain most of the fall; Section 174 can't explain the drop since 2022. Its \"65% of January 2020\" is now stale (see D61).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d62"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U7/F22: BLS CES, computer systems design and related services (NAICS 5415, SA): 2,483.5k in March 2023 (the high of the saved 2022-26 pull) to 2,355.4k in Sept 2026 (preliminary): -128.1k, -5.2% (our arithmetic). Dec 2024 2,431.4k; Dec 2025 2,380.6k. ep46 B44 measured from Dec 2022 (2,483.0k, ChatGPT's launch), not from the peak; both are right. IT-services firms, not in-house IT departments. Quote is JSON (machine-unchecked).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d63"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U58/F22: BLS 2025-35: computer programmers 110,800 jobs, -7% (-8,100); BLS names AI automating repetitive programming.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d64"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U58/F22: software developers, QA analysts and testers 1,905,400, +10% (+185,400), \"much faster than average\"; BLS cites AI development as a source of demand (ep46 T4-11).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d65"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U58: \"computer user support specialists: 903,100, -3% (-24,300)\". Wrong line of the table. 903,100 / -24,300 is all computer support specialists (SOC 15-1230). User support specialists (15-1232) are 750,600, projected 724,400, -3% (-26,200); network support (15-1231) is +1%. The chatbot reason applies to user support.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d66"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U59: Challenger (announced US cuts, reason as the employer states it): AI led reasons in March 2026, 15,341 cuts, 25%; Technology 52,050 in Q1, +40% y/y.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d67"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U59: AI cited for 87,714 cuts (22%) through May; now read from Challenger's own May report (the dossier had only secondary coverage).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d68"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U59: Technology 149,023 cuts through July (Challenger's own July report). By July the AI year-to-date was 112,713 (same report's table).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d69"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U59: Challenger does not record which use of AI drove a cut; its own report calls the AI line ambiguous (ep46 B15). Nothing ties these cuts to business teams building their own tools.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d70"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U31/F22: Gartner (April 2026, read as the Business Wire text via a republication): worldwide IT spending $6.31T in 2026, +13.5%; software $1.44T (+15.1%); IT services $1.87T (+9.0%); data-centre systems +55.8%. Worldwide, not US; Gartner partly credits memory price rises. The release text says \"$1.87 billion\" for IT services; its own table (in millions) gives 1,870,197, i.e. $1.87 trillion. The February 6.15T forecast was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d71"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U79/F22: Recognize CIO survey (200+ US IT executives, released Dec 2025; Recognize is an investment firm focused on tech services, so it has a stake): 85% expect budgets up, 3% down; 67% plan bigger IT teams; 55% expect to replace some commercial software with AI-built tools. Read via trade-press relays of its release.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d72"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U79: who Recognize is.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d73"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"U79: McKinsey State of AI 2026, 32% of organisations skipped buying a software product because they could build it. Not opened (dossier read it secondhand). Do not air without the McKinsey PDF.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-d74"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"F22 headline: \"nothing ties IT or junior-developer job changes to business teams building their own tools\". No source opened in this pass draws that link, and none claims it. This is an absence finding from a limited search, not a measured null.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-d75"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"England, autumn 2020: 15,841 positive Covid cases from 25 September to 2 October were left out of the reported daily figures and reached contact tracing late. PHE's official cause: some lab result files were larger than the maximum file size the loading process could take. Published 4 Oct 2020, updated 5 Oct.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-1"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"The official account says Excel did it.\" → PHE's own statement never names Excel or XLS (zero hits in the saved text); it says \"maximum file size\". The Excel detail comes from BBC reporting (5 Oct 2020): PHE pulled the labs' CSV files into old-format XLS templates capped at about 65,000 rows (about 1,400 cases each). The Health Secretary, per the BBC, called it a \"legacy system\". The peer-reviewed paper (T1-4) also says Excel row limit, \"according to government reports\". Say \"the BBC reported\" or \"researchers found\", not \"the government said Excel\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-2"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Who built it: the XLS step was PHE's own process, built by its own developers. It was not a business team going around IT. It was an official pipeline built on a desktop file format. The case shows data silently dropped in a hand-built step, not shadow IT. Single press source for the \"own developers\" detail.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-3"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"What it cost: Fetzer and Graeber (PNAS, 17 Aug 2021, peer-reviewed) treat the glitch as a natural experiment. About 20% of cases in that window missed timely contact tracing. Each late referral was associated with 18.6 extra reported infections and about 0.24 extra Covid deaths over the next six weeks. This is their regression estimate, not a count.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-4"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The widely repeated \"125,000 extra infections and 1,500 deaths\" from the PHE glitch. Neither figure appears in the saved peer-reviewed text. They may come from an earlier working-paper version, which was not opened. Do not multiply T1-4's per-referral rates by 15,841 on air. The paper's window counts a subset of the late referrals.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-5"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"UK Department for Transport, West Coast Main Line franchise (2012). DfT used a model built for a different job: the \"GDP Resilience Model\" was made to calibrate a revenue-sharing mechanism, not to size bidders' subordinated loan facilities. Its outputs were in real terms and officials did not inflate them to nominal, so the numbers were understated by nearly half. The competition was cancelled on 3 Oct 2012. Official inquiry (Laidlaw, Dec 2012).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-6"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Cost of the West Coast cancellation per the NAO (7 Dec 2012): £8.9m to date in staff, advisers, preparing for judicial review and the follow-up reviews. Bidders' later compensation is NOT in this figure and was not checked here. Inquiry blame is wider than the model: planning, organisation, governance, and inaccurate reports to ministers (Laidlaw).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-7"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Barclays / Lehman, September 2008: a 1,000-row spreadsheet of trading contracts was converted to PDF for the bankruptcy court. Rows hidden because they were NOT part of the deal came back into view, so the offer took on 179 contracts Barclays never meant to buy. Found 1 Oct, after the judge approved the sale. Barclays moved to amend. The financial effect was unclear. Press relay (The Register, citing Finextra and Above the Law); the court motion was not opened. Don't name the junior lawyer (a private individual).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-8"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"A measured rate of quiet data corruption in hand-built files: Ziemann, Eren and El-Osta (Genome Biology, 23 Aug 2016, peer-reviewed) screened 35,175 supplementary Excel files from 18 journals (2005-2015). In about one-fifth of papers with Excel gene lists (704 of 3,597), the gene names had been converted into dates or numbers by Excel's default settings. Scientists, not business users. It is the one prevalence measurement found of a desktop tool silently changing the data people rely on.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-9"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"A chatbot that contradicted the company's own source of truth: Air Canada's website chatbot told a customer he could claim a bereavement fare after travel. The policy page it linked to said the opposite. BC's Civil Resolution Tribunal (Moffatt v Air Canada, 2024 BCCRT 149, 14 Feb 2024) held the airline responsible for everything on its site, chatbot or static page. Damages were just over C$650. ⚠ The decision itself was NOT opened (CanLII 403); this rests on a law-firm summary and Global News. It is a customer-facing tool, and who built it is not in the record. Don't name the customer.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-10"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Regulators fined a bank for data-quality failures: on 10 July 2024 the Fed fined Citigroup $60.6m and the OCC fined Citibank $75m. Both cited insufficient progress on data quality management under their 2020 orders; the OCC also cited no process to monitor how data quality affected regulatory reporting. ⚠ This is enterprise data governance at a global bank, NOT business-built tools. Use it only as \"regulators put a price on bad data\", never as a shadow-IT case.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-11"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Beyond the deletion (B44): did the Replit agent also fabricate data? Yes, by the founder's own account as relayed by The Register on 21 July 2025. He said the agent covered up bugs by creating fake data and fake reports and lied about a unit test. His X posts themselves were not opened. B44's follow-up piece (22 July) also says \"invented data\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-12"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The \"4,000 fake users\": per The Register, in a LinkedIn video (not opened) the founder said the agent created a 4,000-record database full of fictional people. The saved text doesn't say whether those records sat in the live app or were shown to anyone as real. Say \"a database of 4,000 people who didn't exist\", attributed to him.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-13"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The agent also gave false information about recovery. It said rollback could not restore the database; it could. Founder's account, relayed. Context for the script: he was testing a build over about ten days, and no business was running on that app (a trial build, not an enterprise deployment).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-14"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Any other documented case of an AI-built business app running in production on mock, test or hallucinated data, or any count of how often that happens. We found no documented case other than Replit and no measurement in the sources opened. Scanner reports (B35-B43) count exposed apps and secrets, not fake data.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-15"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"Gartner: poor data quality costs organisations $12.9m a year.\" → Origin traced: Gartner's Magic Quadrant for Data Quality Solutions (27 July 2020). The figure is what organisations ESTIMATED in Gartner's survey of vendor-nominated reference customers: 154 organisations tied to 16 data-quality vendors. It is not a measured cost and not a representative sample. The report doesn't say how many answered the cost question. Gartner.com itself was not reachable; the report was read at a licensed reprint (located through a secondary guide). Air only as \"data-quality software customers estimated\" or not at all.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-16"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"Bad data costs the US $3.1 trillion a year\" (often credited to IBM). → The earliest version found is a 2011 press release giving one consultant's estimate, with his own caveat. Whether IBM later adopted it (HBR, 2016) was not checked: the HBR body is paywalled. Don't air.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-17"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Nagle, Redman and Sammon (HBR, 11 Sep 2017): 75 executives in Irish executive programmes each checked 100 of their own department's recent records (\"Friday Afternoon Measurement\"). On average 47% of newly created records had at least one critical error; 3% of scores were \"acceptable\". Small, self-run sample. It measures errors in records, not which source a tool used.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-18"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"How often business-built tools pull from the wrong or unofficial source (a stale export, a personal copy, the wrong join) rather than the system of record. We found no measurement in the sources opened: no regulator, academic or vendor count. The nearest are T1-9 (corruption by a tool's defaults), T1-18 (record errors) and supervisors' bank-level findings (T1-25, T1-27).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-19"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"SR 11-7 (Fed and OCC, 4 April 2011; FDIC adopted it in 2017) demanded rigorous checks of a model's data and warned that user-built spreadsheets and ad hoc databases are \"particularly prone to model risk\". Read from the FDIC's adoption copy.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-20"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"SR 11-7 is the current US rule on bank models\" (dossier's gap list; brief item 7). → It is not. On 17 April 2026 the Fed, OCC and FDIC replaced SR 11-7 (and SR 21-8) with SR 26-2, \"Revised Guidance on Model Risk Management\". It is aimed mainly at banks over $30bn in assets.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-21"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"★ The new US guidance narrows the scope just where business-built tools live. It excludes \"simple arithmetic calculations, such as those found within spreadsheets\" and rule-based software. It puts generative and agentic AI models outside its scope and leaves them to each bank's own governance. The \"user-developed applications ... particularly prone\" sentence is gone (zero hits for \"user-developed\" or \"ad hoc database\" in either SR 26-2 file).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-22"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"What SR 26-2 keeps on data: testing may include a critical look at data quality and inputs, scaled to how complex and material the model is. \"May\", not \"must\"; the old \"rigorous assessment\" wording is softened.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-23"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"UK contrast: the PRA's SS1/23 (May 2023, in force 17 May 2024) explicitly lists end-user computing applications among the \"quantitative methods\" whose use in decisions should be tested for correct implementation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-24"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"BCBS 239 (Basel Committee, January 2013), Principle 3. Banks relying on spreadsheets and desktop databases need end-user computing controls. Risk data must be reconciled with source and accounting data. Banks should \"strive towards a single authoritative source\" per risk type, with a shared data dictionary. Global systemically important banks (G-SIBs) were to comply by January 2016. Written for banks' risk data, not all business tools.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-25"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"How it went: the Basel Committee's November 2023 progress report found only 2 of 31 G-SIBs fully compliant, nearly ten years on and seven years past the deadline. No principle was fully met across all banks. In 2020, none were compliant. Causes named: fragmented IT, legacy systems, manual processes; one bank's reports leaned on \"end-user compensating controls\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-26"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"ECB Guide on risk data aggregation and risk reporting (May 2024, \"minimum supervisory expectations\" for euro-area significant banks). It defines data lineage (tracing data from capture to report, so a bad number can be traced to its source). It requires end-user-built applications to be listed and brought into data-quality management. Manual workarounds get four-eyes checks until moved into an audit-trailed, IT-controlled environment. Its thematic review found none of 25 significant institutions fully followed BCBS 239.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-27"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"US bank supervisors (Federal Reserve and OCC, SR 11-7, 4 April 2011) expect a firm-wide inventory of models in use, in development or recently retired, kept by a named party.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-1"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"SR 11-7's inventory should record the source of a model's inputs and the names of the people responsible. This is the \"where does the data come from, and who\" question, asked by a regulator in 2011.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-2"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"SR 11-7 says documentation must let a stranger run the model, and says outright that documentation is what provides continuity. It also predicts the builder's blind spot.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-3"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Scope trap for T2-1 to T2-3: SR 11-7 is supervisory guidance for banks, and it covers models (quantitative estimating methods), not every business-built tool. A team's sign-up app is not a \"model\".","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-4"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The UK's bank regulator (PRA, SS1/23, in force 17 May 2024) names end-user computing applications by name among the quantitative methods banks use day to day.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-5"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"SS1/23 wants a complete firm-wide model inventory, including decommissioned models. Tools that are not models still need \"clearly documented\" controls.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-6"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Scope trap: SS1/23 binds only UK banks, building societies and designated investment firms that have internal-model approval. Others are only \"welcome to consider\" it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-7"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The general security baseline (CIS Critical Security Control 2) says to inventory and control all software and to find \"unmanaged\" software. Checked wording: it is about software \"on the network\" that is \"installed\", so a web app built on an outside platform fits it only loosely. (The CIS Controls are a voluntary framework, not a law.)","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-8"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Internal audit's guide to user-developed applications (IIA GTAG 14, 2010) says what auditors should require.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t2-9"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The FFIEC IT Examination Handbook's guidance on end-user development, for US bank examiners.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t2-10"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The UK's bank regulator fined Metro Bank £5,376,000 (final notice dated 21 December 2021) over regulatory reporting of its capital, for returns sent 13 May 2016 to 23 January 2019.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-11"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The final notice names key-person dependency on spreadsheets as a failing, in a regulator's own words. Checking was manual and depended on the few people who understood the sheets.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-12"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The documentation problem in the same notice: where the bank wrote down how it read the rules, that record was buried inside spreadsheets.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-13"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Scale of the Metro Bank error: the risk-weighted-assets correction was about £900 million. Trap: the fine is for reporting governance as a whole. It is NOT a story about a builder who left, and the notice does not say anyone left. It is the dependency before anyone leaves.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t2-14"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The one-person-holds-the-keys case with a court-documented cost: San Francisco's principal network engineer (2003 to July 2008) was the only person with administrative access to the city's FiberWAN network.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-15"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"When he was reassigned and refused to hand over access, the city was locked out from 9 to 21 July 2008. Nobody, including outside experts, could get in until he gave up the codes. The network kept running throughout.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-16"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The bill: $1,485,791 in restitution. About $380,000 went on regaining control and about $1.1m on a new city-wide security system and on checking whether he still had access. Affirmed on appeal.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-17"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"A telling detail for the leaver story: the defence argued the $1.1m clean-up would have been spent even if he had handed over access and simply resigned (the court did not accept it as a reason to cut the order). Trap: this was an IT employee convicted of a crime, not a business user whose tool was orphaned. Air it only as an analogy, and never by name.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-18"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Access that outlives the job: Block disclosed (8-K, 4 April 2022) that a former employee downloaded Cash App Investing reports on 10 December 2021, after their employment ended, using access their old job had given them. About 8.2 million current and former customers were contacted. The gap between the download and the disclosure was about four months.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-19"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Trap for T2-19: the reports held names, brokerage account numbers and some portfolio data, not Social Security numbers or passwords. It is a leaver-access case, not a business-built tool.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-20"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"A documented case where a business-built (non-IT) tool or vibe-coded app was orphaned when its builder left, with a stated recovery or replacement cost (audit finding, regulator order, court record or post-incident report).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t2-21"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Google says it outright for Apps Script, the scripts that sit behind many business-built Sheets tools: when the owner leaves and their account is removed, access to a script nobody else owns is lost.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-22"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Google's fix is a shared drive, where files have no individual owner. It is only on paid Business and Enterprise tiers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-23"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"An Apps Script automation runs as the person who created it, so the tool acts in the builder's name. Whether it stops when that account is deleted is not stated on the page.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-24"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"When a Google Workspace account is deleted, anything the user alone owned that was not transferred is permanently deleted. The window to restore the user is 20 days.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-25"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Microsoft Power Automate: a flow's creator becomes its owner automatically, and for flows outside a \"solution\" the owner \"is part of the flow identity\". It cannot be reassigned in place, only rebuilt or copied.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-26"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Microsoft's admin centre lists Power Apps with no valid owner and calls them a business continuity risk. Checked scope: only apps in \"managed environments\" that were active in the last 90 days. A new owner does not inherit access to the app's data sources. So \"Microsoft flags every orphaned app\" is wrong.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t2-27"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The spreadsheet in the leaver's OneDrive: by default it is deleted 30 days after the account is deleted. The manager gets access only if one is set in the directory. Otherwise \"no one\" has automatic access, or is even warned.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-28"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Lovable, inside a company workspace: when a project owner leaves, projects pass automatically to the highest-ranked member. Checked exception: projects set to Restricted are not transferred and need a manual hand-over. So \"the app dies with the builder\" is wrong for a company workspace.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t2-29"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Lovable's company controls (verified domains, SSO, auto-provisioning) are on the Business and Enterprise plans, and SCIM is Enterprise only. \"Domain lock\" is not a self-serve switch: two of its settings are turned on by Lovable support. Even then, accounts staff already made \"keep working as before\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-30"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Lovable can pull existing accounts that use the company's email domain into the workspace (\"Add existing users\"), and can block new personal workspaces on that domain. The page does not say that projects already built in a person's personal workspace move with them.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t2-31"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Lovable's terms: an account opened with an employer's email \"may\" be treated as managed by that employer. It is permissive, not automatic.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-32"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Replit, personal account: deleting it removes every app on it, and nothing can be recovered.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-33"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Replit: moving a personal app into the company's team workspace is something the builder does, from their own account, after joining the team. It is irreversible, and the app's linked account secrets do not travel with it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-34"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Replit's company sign-on (SAML SSO; Enterprise) forces staff on the company's domain to sign in through SSO, but existing users \"aren't automatically added\" to the company. Public domains like gmail.com cannot be claimed, so an app built on a personal Gmail account is outside it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-35"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"What Bolt (or other vibe-coding hosts not read here) does with a departed member's projects.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t2-36"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Whether a Power Apps canvas app or a non-solution flow keeps running after its owner's account is deleted. Microsoft's pages read here say \"business continuity risk\" and that the owner is part of the flow identity, but they do not say \"it stops\".","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t2-37"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Microsoft's shadow-IT discovery works by matching network traffic logs against a catalogue of 31,000+ known cloud services. Checked limit: by default it cannot see apps that are not in that catalogue. So \"IT can see every app staff use\" is wrong. Our inference, not Microsoft's words: seeing traffic to a platform is not the same as knowing which app was built there, or what data it holds.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t2-38"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The discovery is fed by logs \"from your network\" (firewalls, proxies, a log collector), with Defender for Endpoint extending it \"beyond your corporate network\" on enrolled devices. Our inference: a builder on a personal laptop at home, on a personal account, sits outside both.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-39"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Connecting an outside app to company data can be one employee's click. Out of the box in Microsoft Entra, any user can approve an app's access to their own data (for example their mailbox) without an admin. Microsoft recommends tightening this.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-40"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Google's own leaver checklist tells admins to review all authorised access and revoke \"any other authorized applications\". That is a manual step. Changing the password revokes only some tokens.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-41"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"VENDOR SURVEY (ClusterSeven, a seller of spreadsheet-control software, 2016, nearly 160 UK internal-audit, finance, compliance and risk staff): 88% recognise spreadsheet and EUC risk, only 24% enforce manual controls, and more than 60% rely on spreadsheets for business-critical processes. Old, small, UK-only, and it measures attitudes, not orphaned tools.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-42"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"A measurement of how many business-built tools (spreadsheets, low-code, vibe-coded apps) are orphaned or undocumented, or how often IT inherits one when the builder leaves.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t2-43"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Cross-reference, not re-checked: failing to inventory is already a documented, paid-for failure (Wegmans, $400,000, A42). California's coming audit asks for software inventories (A31). Microsoft's orphan clean-up sits in a kit that is no longer maintained (C28, C29).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t2-44"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"UK NCSC guidance \"Shadow IT\" (published 27 July 2023, reviewed 14 August 2026): shadow IT is rarely malicious; it is normally staff struggling with sanctioned tools; a slow or ineffective request process is one of its listed causes, alongside missing tools and approved tools lacking functionality (AI for admin tasks is its example). A list of causes, not a measurement.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-1"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The same NCSC guidance treats shadow IT as a signal that policy needs fixing: answer requests quickly, give a controlled fast lane to services outside the normal set, migrate what is found onto supported platforms, and never blame the staff. This is the closest any government body read comes to describing the governed middle (C31 has the NCSC's separate tiers for vibe coding itself).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-2"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(vendor) MuleSoft Connectivity Benchmark, Salesforce press release 10 Feb 2017: of 951 IT decision makers (orgs of 500+ staff, nine countries, surveyed 16–29 Nov 2016), only half said they delivered every project asked of them the previous year; 51% said they were under-resourced. IT leaders reporting on themselves; an integration vendor's survey.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-3"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(vendor) MuleSoft 2020 Connectivity Benchmark (with Vanson Bourne, 800 IT leaders, orgs of 1,000+): 59% could not deliver all projects the year before; project workloads up 40%. Read only through a trade-site reprint of the press release, not at MuleSoft.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-4"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(vendor-commissioned) Economist Intelligence Unit for Appian, \"IT's changing mandate in an age of disruption\" (relay dated 29 Nov 2021): average backlog for planned IT projects three to 12 months; over half say business units \"do more\" than IT to procure or develop apps. The relay is tagged as promoted content, gives no sample size or method, and the report itself was not read. The only wait-time figure found.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-5"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(vendor) NTT Com / Vanson Bourne, March 2016, 500 IT and business decision makers at firms of 1,000+ in the UK, France, Germany and Spain: the reasons given are ease of set-up (62%) and ease of use (52%), more than speed; 77% said stopping shadow IT would limit their department; 56% had no idea where shadow-IT data was stored. Europe, not the US; ten years old.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-6"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(vendor) Ivanti survey, 13 May 2015, 275+ respondents, 70% IT managers. Checked version: the page's own \"Key Takeaways\" summary names IT resource constraints as a main driver, but its data list puts \"IT can't meet a department timeline\" LAST, at 9%; the top reason is that SaaS is easier to get (73%). It also finds 92% of IT teams inherited shadow-IT projects (7.1 on average), which bears on T2 (the leaver).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t3-7"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(vendor-commissioned) K2 / Harris Poll, 700+ US office workers, reported by CIO (13 Mar 2015): two in five workers say IT is too inundated to fill requests quickly. Commissioned by a business-apps vendor; read only in the article.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-8"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(vendor) GigaOM Research for CipherCloud, reported 17 Nov 2014: 38% of employees deliberately bypassed IT because approval was slow. Relay only; no sample stated. Old and about cloud apps, not building.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-9"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(vendor) Retool 2026 Build vs. Buy report (17 Feb 2026), 817 of Retool's own customers and builders, late 2025, 36% software engineers: 60% built something outside IT oversight in the past year; asked why, 31% said speed, 25% unmet needs, 18% IT's process too slow. The freshest stated-reasons figure, from a vendor's own user base, which sells the governed alternative.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-10"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(vendor, self-report) Same survey on what the tools deliver: 51% shipped production software their teams use, and about half of those (49%) say it saves six or more hours a week. Also: only 44% test thoroughly before deploying, and 8% use AI code without changes. A self-estimate of hours, not a measurement.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-11"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"GSA Inspector General, report A210057/B/5/F24001 (30 Nov 2023): GSA claimed its 70+ bots \"reclaim more than 240,000 work hours every year\"; the IG found GSA never checked actual hours with users and called the claim inaccurate and unreliable. In a sample of 10 high-claim bots, 9 varied from estimate: one estimated at 3,650 hours saved no time; two were retired after four months; one assumed 90 minutes for a 30-minute task. The independent check on \"time saved\". These were bots built by GSA's central RPA office from estimates supplied by each bot's process owner, not tools business staff built themselves.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-12"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Federal RPA Community of Practice, \"State of Federal RPA\" (Dec 2021): 1,001 automations in production. Checked version: digital.gov summarises this as \"freeing up almost 1.5 million hours\", but the report's figure covers \"current and potential\" automations (the pipeline is \"just as many\"), is cumulative, and is self-reported in a voluntary survey of 49 programmes.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t3-13"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Microsoft Inside Track, 15 Nov 2023: Microsoft's case for letting staff build is the person who \"can't access the formal engineering resources\"; it reports 1 million+ citizen-built assets internally and one tool (Cosmic, revenue processing) at about $14.2M a year in savings. Microsoft on its own product, no method for the figure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-14"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Stripe, \"Harbor\" (stripe.dev, 14 Sep 2026): since May 2026, 3,000+ staff made 12,000 prototypes and 25% of staff made at least one; finance, risk operations and account executives built their own (monthly actuals, a handle-time dashboard, pricing proposals, ROI calculators). Adoption counts, not outcomes.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-15"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Ramp, \"Why We Built Our Own Background Agent\" (12 Jan 2026): its agent Inspect writes ~30% of PRs merged to its frontend and backend repos, unmandated. The post does not say what share comes from non-engineers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-16"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"1,500 internal apps shipped in six weeks; 12% of production code from non-engineers at Ramp.\" Said by the HOST of a podcast (State of Play, 28 Apr 2026) in his introduction, attributed to Ramp with no source. Not found on any Ramp page reached (blog index, Inspect post, Laminar post, Glass page). Do not air.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t3-17"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Anthropic, \"How AI is transforming work at Anthropic\" (2 Dec 2025): 132 of its engineers and researchers estimated 27% of their Claude-assisted work would not have been done otherwise (\"nice-to-have tools\", \"papercuts\"). The long tail no one would prioritise, but engineers estimating their own work, not business staff. Disclosure rule: a plain fact labelled as Anthropic's.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-18"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim, anecdotes) Anthropic, \"How Anthropic teams use Claude Code\" (24 Jul 2025): its Legal team prototyped a \"phone tree\" to route staff to the right lawyer; Growth Marketing built a workflow that generates \"hundreds of new ads in minutes instead of hours\". No measurement behind either.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-19"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(analyst survey) Gartner 2025 CIO survey (22 Oct 2024; 3,186 CIOs, 1,126 non-IT executives): only 48% of digital initiatives meet their targets; the \"digital vanguard\", who CO-OWN delivery with the business, hit 71%; on average 26% of business-area staff outside IT build, implement or manage technology. An association in a survey, not proof that co-ownership causes success.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-20"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Alloway & Quillard, \"User managers' systems needs\" (MIT working paper 1982; MIS Quarterly 1983), the classic academic measurement of an \"invisible backlog\" of user demand. Only the DSpace record was saved; the PDF was refused twice (429) and no figure was read.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t3-21"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Gartner's own current glossary definitions of \"citizen developer\" and \"fusion team\". Both pages returned a tracker shell or 403; not read. The 2009 definition stands at C19.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t3-22"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Microsoft Inside Track, 13 Feb 2025, how Microsoft governs its OWN staff's Power Platform building: three tiers. Personal productivity: no sharing, one owner, self-service, deleted after 90 days idle. Team collaboration: tied to a Microsoft 365 group, deleted after 90 days idle. Enterprise: request and approval, at least two owners of whom one is a full-time employee, owners attest every six months, registered in Service Tree, twice-yearly security, privacy and accessibility reviews. The most complete published owner model found.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-23"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Same post: inside Microsoft, every environment has one accountable owner, and staff are auto-routed OUT of the default environment. Contrast C27: in the product as shipped, every employee can reach the default environment. Microsoft's own practice is stricter than its product's default.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-24"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Same post, the RUN stage after launch: every six months owners confirm they still own the asset, use no unauthorised data and share nothing outside the tenant; an automated monitor emails owners when a configuration drifts from policy.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-25"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Microsoft Inside Track, 15 Nov 2023: every staff-built solution gets an internal risk rating that sets its compliance review. Simple Teams/SharePoint connectors in personal productivity go live with no permission; a Dataverse connector needs a permission request first. Microsoft's own staff name the risk: apps in business use with no IT, security or privacy oversight.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-26"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Microsoft Inside Track, \"Governing AI agents at scale\" (21 May 2026), the RETIRE stage: agents owned by one employee are deleted when that person leaves; team-owned agents get a lifecycle tied to attestation and the internal SDLC; enterprise agents go through the same SDLC reviews as other enterprise software. By this policy, a one-person tool dies with its builder's departure.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-27"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(analyst recommendation) Gartner, \"Govern Vibe Coding for Citizen Developers With Self-Service Platforms\" (29 Jun 2026), the four-zone framework. Green: personal or small-group, low criticality, with automated security scanning. Yellow: more complex team tools; if they grow in value they must be handed to engineering. Red: internal, complex AND critical, not to be vibe-coded by citizen developers. Black: customer-facing or holding customer data, no citizen development with any tool, full engineering ownership. Hand pass C quoted only this note's forecasts (C21).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-28"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(analyst recommendation) Same note, who decides: the builder self-assesses (number of users, data classification, business impact, internal vs external users); a platform team validates and reviews within one to two days (green) or three to five (yellow); engineering leadership retires the low-value and abandoned and takes over the high-value. This puts a WAIT TIME into the governed model itself.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-29"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Stripe Harbor (14 Sep 2026), the guardrail is the DATA: one engineer built it in about 40 days so designers could prototype \"without needing to understand code\", on Stripe's design systems and realistic MOCK data; one published version at a time; the author controls who may comment or remix. Live data is listed as future work.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-30"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Stripe Harbor, the hand-off from build to RUN: when an internal tool is worth keeping, engineers rebuild it for production with a coding agent; Harbor's tooling passes the prototype into a real repository and keeps \"the decisions around ownership and implementation\". Anyone builds the prototype; engineers own what goes to production.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-31"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Stripe Minions (9 Feb 2026): 1,000+ merged PRs a week are written entirely by agents, but every one is human-reviewed, and the agents run on devboxes cut off from production and the internet. The post describes ENGINEERS using it; it is not a non-engineer platform.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-32"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"Non-engineers at Stripe use minions to ship code.\" Only an episode-topic line on Lenny's Newsletter (25 Mar 2026, a Stripe engineer as guest); the content was not read and Stripe's own post doesn't say it.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t3-33"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Ramp Inspect (12 Jan 2026): built so \"builders of all backgrounds\" (product managers, designers) can contribute, with one control spelled out: the agent opens pull requests under the user's own GitHub token, so nobody can approve their own change and unreviewed code cannot slip in.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-34"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Ramp \"Glass\", per The Pragmatic Engineer (Gergely Orosz, a newsletter, published 15 Oct 2026, after visiting the four-person Glass team): a vibe-coding platform for non-engineers building internal apps on internal \"connectors\"; part of new-hire onboarding; \"pretty much every non-engineer\" uses it. The writer's rationale: an in-house tool makes it safer to expose internal data. Stripe's in-house equivalent is Harbor. A journalist's account of what Ramp told him; Ramp has published no write-up, and its Glass page is a marketing stub.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-35"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Ramp's head of design (an executive in role, State of Play podcast, 28 Apr 2026), asked \"Who's governing glass?\": for internal tools, everyone decides (\"chaos\"); customer-facing products have a quality bar before general release. The only Ramp statement on governance found, and it describes a high-trust, ungoverned internal layer. Spoken; read from the page's transcript, which is machine-made (it misspells his name once).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t3-36"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(company claim) Ramp, \"Building Laminar\" (20 Oct 2026), its in-house agent platform for its own products: even when coding agents make building cheap, someone must be ON-CALL. That is Ramp's platform engineers for the platform, not owners of staff-built apps.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-37"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Who is on call for an app a non-engineer built on Glass or Harbor, and who retires it? Neither company's published material reached says. Microsoft (T3-23, T3-27) and Gartner (T3-29) are the only sources found that assign the run and retire stages.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t3-38"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"Federal RPA (Dec 2021, self-reported): 41% of programmes run centralised governance, 27% federated, 18% decentralised. At GSA, a central RPA office in the CFO's office builds and manages the bots, and each bot's \"process owner, often a supervisor\" supplies the time estimate (the estimate the IG found unreliable, T3-12).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-39"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"(agency claim, self-reported) Navy NAVSUP Business Systems Center, a named federated model that brings in citizen developers: \"RPA 101\" for nearly 100 staff, 20+ citizen developers onboarded, a central portal, bot templates, and a governance team; claims 14,000+ labour hours saved (unverified estimate, cf. T3-12).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-40"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"NCSC (C31) and the Five Eyes agentic-AI guidance (C33) on what should be allowed: full vibe coding can be fine for prototypes and limited-exposure internal tools; agents only for low-risk, non-sensitive tasks. Cited, not redone. Together with Gartner's zones (T3-28) and Microsoft's tiers (T3-23), four independent sources draw the same line: by what the tool touches.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t3-41"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The peg has not moved: Anthropic's registration is still the Form S-1 of 2026-10-26. No amendment (S-1/A), no pricing prospectus (424B) and no ticker or exchange appear on EDGAR, so the IPO has not priced and rows P1–P13 stand as written. Read 2026-10-28.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f1"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"No new incident filing names an AI tool. EDGAR full-text search of 8-Ks filed 2026-10-01 to 2026-10-28: \"Item 1.05\" + \"artificial intelligence\" (1 hit, a board committee charter, items 5.02/5.05/8.01, not an incident); \"Material Cybersecurity Incidents\" + \"artificial intelligence\" (2 hits, the same charter and a spin-off exhibit, neither an incident); \"unauthorized\" + \"artificial intelligence\" + \"cybersecurity incident\" (8 hits, none filed under Item 1.05: earnings, agreements and other events; the one Item 8.01 among them is an office move and a compliance policy); \"shadow IT\", \"AI-generated code\", and \"AI tool\" + \"cybersecurity incident\" (0 hits each). The only Item 1.05 filing in the window, Park Dental Partners (2026-10-13), describes outside unauthorized access to its network and does not mention AI. So CB Financial (A8–A14) is still the only such filing found, now searched through 2026-10-28. Limit: the search sees only literal wording.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f2"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The definition of vibe coding the script uses comes from the UK's cyber agency (the same blog as C31): giving an AI agent a high-level prompt and letting it build the application with significant autonomy. The same page calls an internal team tool with limited risk \"maybe … manageable\" before saying full vibe coding \"in these contexts can often be perfectly fine\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f3"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"The bank's filing has no follow-up that changes the script's past-tense line (\"the root cause was still under investigation\"). EDGAR lists no amendment (8-K/A) to CB Financial's Item 1.05 filing of 2026-05-11; its later 8-Ks (2026-05-20, 07-27, 08-25, 09-16, 10-26) are a shareholder vote, dividends, earnings, a buyback and an officer change. The primary documents of the three Item 8.01 filings (07-27, 09-16, 10-26) were read and none mentions the incident; the earnings-release exhibits were not read. Read 2026-10-28.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f12"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"The bank learned of it on May fifth, judged it material two days later, and filed on May eleventh\": 5 May aware (A8), 7 May material (A9) = two days later; filed 11 May (A12), two business days after the materiality call.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f4"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"Nearly sixteen thousand positive Covid cases\": 15,841 (T1-1), rounded.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f5"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"About six in ten … worked, and about one in eight were secure\": 57% functionally correct = 5.7 in 10; 11.8% secure = 1 in 8.5 (1 in 8 would be 12.5%). Both rounded (B31).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f6"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"The code runs almost every time, but it passes the security checks only about half the time\": syntax correct over 95% of the time; security pass rate about 55% (B20), in a vendor's lab tests of raw models.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f7"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"For about eleven weeks this year\": 3 February to 20 April 2026 (B49) is 76 days, 10.9 weeks.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f8"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"Among organisations that had a breach … more than doubled in a year, from one in five to more than four in ten\": 20% in IBM's 2025 study (B1) to 43% in 2026 (B6). \"More than doubled\" is IBM's wording; the rounding is ours.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f9"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"In more than a third of US firms where workers use AI\": 36% (D9), rounded.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f10"},{"s":"ep48-vibe-coding-and-shadow-it","n":48,"c":"\"Fined Metro Bank over five million pounds\": £5,376,000 (T2-11), rounded down.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f11"}]);